// Every constraint takes `message:` to override its text, as every Jakarta constraint does -- and
// it is **named**, so a constraint that reads as though it took one bare argument is refused
// rather than guessed at. `#[min(8)]` still works, because Java's own convention is that a lone
// value is the constraint's main one.
struct E {
    #[not_blank("say why")]
    name: string
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
